      "event_id": "handover-checklist",
      "project": "garden",
      "topic": "dev-handover-checklist",
      "language": "en",
      "title": "Sequoia handover notes",
      "content": "Sequoia is a fictional team preparing a monthly delivery. Its coordinator first lists all open tasks and marks which outputs have an identified recipient. During the meeting, each owner can describe a difficulty without treating it as a completed result. The team keeps links to exact artifacts so a later reader can distinguish a proposal from a verified output.\n\nThe handover page has a section for context. This section explains why a task was requested, which assumptions were used and which documents are authoritative. It avoids copying the entire discussion because a long transcript makes the actual decision hard to find. An old estimate remains a historical estimate until someone measures the current result.\n\nThe coordinator then checks dependencies. A task can be finished locally while another team still has to activate it. In that situation, the note identifies the completed local layer and names the separate owner of activation. The coordinator never marks the external step complete based on the producer's intention.\n\nDuring a review, reviewers can open the linked output, compare it with the stated requirement and record a concrete observation. A disagreement is attached to the relevant part of the artifact instead of becoming a general judgment about the contributor. Corrections keep the previous receipt available so a later reader can understand what changed.\n\nThe last section is the operational handover rule. Sequoia maintains one handover checklist. A task owner records the result, links the exact output, names a remaining limitation and assigns the next owner. A green test badge alone does not complete the handover."
    },
